rs to make some  progress against a trio of massive fires  but crews are now bracing for a weather  system that's expected to bring more  high winds and lightning storms flames  have already destroyed nearly one  thousand homes and other structures  and tens of thousands have been forced  to leave the fires burning on all sides  of the san francisco bay area  were started by lightning strikes over  the past week  and hurricane warnings have now been  declared along the u.s gulf coast  as thousands brace for back to back  blows from  two powerful storms tropical storm marco  powered into a category 1 hurricane  today  and laura is also picking up steam in  the caribbean as both track towards the  u.s  hurricane marco is forecast to slam into  texas and southeastern louisiana  tomorrow  while laura is likely to strengthen into  a hurricane and hit late wednesday  or early thursday we've heard the  warnings of how climate change is  melting glaciers in the arctic but a new  report warns the impact is being felt  much closer to home  alberta's rivers are a key source of  drinking water for the province  and as glaciers shrink so does the water  supply  a new study has identified the  communities most at risk  and jordan armstrong reports it includes  one of the country's most  popular tourist destinations lake louise  is world  famous for its mountains and emerald  waters  but when it comes to the drinking water  the community could be in for a future  of supply problems  that's according to a geophysics phd  candidate  who spent three years studying alberta's  vanishing glaciers  what the tourists will be seeing when  they look at those mountains  is mountains that have less snow less  ice cover  um and then when they're looking at the  river they'll see that in august it will  be lower on average  the town of lake louise gets its water  from the glacier-fed bow river  with less runoff in august anderson says  the drinking supply becomes  inconsistent his study found h